@@ -17,8 +17,11 @@ Direct HTTP/1.1 on `$HOST:$PORT` (no Nginx), written in plain Objective-C on the
 - One SQLite connection (WAL, `synchronous=NORMAL`, no mmap, a 256 MB page cache limit (it holds ~50 MB after startup and ~110 MB after a 5-minute hold), persistent prepared statements). Each loop iteration runs its whole completion batch (all rings) in one transaction (group commit) and submits every response only after `COMMIT` succeeds; if a commit fails, the batch's connections are dropped, so no 201 is ever sent for an uncommitted row.
 - Checkpoints run in a child process, forked at startup before any SQLite or io_uring state exists. The loop asks it (over a pipe, answer read through io_uring) for a `PASSIVE` checkpoint every 2,000 new WAL frames, so the copy and its two fsyncs never block the loop. Under constant writes a passive pass can never copy the whole WAL, so the WAL could not rewind and would grow without bound. When it reaches 8192 frames (~32 MB), writes wait until one pass copies everything; the next write then rewinds the WAL. Reads are answered during that wait. A write, and every later request on the same connection, stays at the front of the batch until the wait ends, so pipelined responses keep their order. The wait is ~40–80 ms every ~1.5 s with an 11 ms-fsync disk, with a 250 ms cap. If the child dies, SQLite's own autocheckpoint takes over inline (every 250 frames). The second process needs the shared-memory WAL index, so locking is `NORMAL`, not `EXCLUSIVE`: the extra file locks measured as noise (11.56 vs 11.54–11.60 µs/req).
 - At the memory wall the kernel evicts file pages first, and a loop that then reads the database from disk stops answering while requests pile up in sockets at 3-4 KB each. Pages in SQLite's own cache are anonymous memory, so they stay. At startup the server reads the `likes` unique index, `posts_user_id_idx` and `users` into that cache (`--warm`), since inserts touch them at random. The checkpointer drops the file pages it has synced (`posix_fadvise`, `--ckdrop`). `journal_size_limit=0` truncates the WAL at the first commit after a rewind, so later frames append instead of overwriting old file pages. That first commit carries a single write (`--onewrite`), because its frames still overwrite old pages, and each page already evicted is read from disk before a partial write. The code and libraries are `mlock`ed within `RLIMIT_MEMLOCK` (`--lock`).
-- Read first, answer later. Each pass reads every completion but answers at most 128 requests; the rest stay parked in a queue. A parked request costs a 64-byte record, while one left in its socket costs a kernel buffer. Sends go out at the end of a pass, so a request waits one to two passes. At 512 answers and 100 % CPU a pass took about 25 ms, which puts half the replies past the kernel's 40 ms delayed-ACK timer (next point). After a burst empties the buffer ring (1 KB × 1,024 per ring), a pass re-arms only as many receives as there are free buffers, oldest first (`--rearm`), since re-arming them all only ends them again with `ENOBUFS`.
+- Read first, answer later. Each pass reads every completion but answers at most 64 requests (`--answers=64` in `start.sh`, 128 without it); the rest stay parked in a queue. A parked request costs a 64-byte record, while one left in its socket costs a kernel buffer. Sends go out at the end of a pass, so a request waits one to two passes. At 512 answers and 100 % CPU a pass took about 25 ms, which puts half the replies past the kernel's 40 ms delayed-ACK timer (next point). After a burst empties the buffer ring (1 KB × 1,024 per ring), a pass re-arms only as many receives as there are free buffers, oldest first (`--rearm`), since re-arming them all only ends them again with `ENOBUFS`.
 - Newest first in a backlog (`--lifo`). A reply sent more than 40 ms after its request costs the kernel an extra bare ACK, about a fifth more CPU per request on our test box. Oldest-first service makes every newcomer that late too once a backlog exists, and the extra CPU can keep that backlog alive at a load the server otherwise handles. So when more than one pass of requests is parked, the newest go first and the older ones wait for spare capacity. Waiting has a bound: from 8 s the oldest get half of each pass (`--lifoage`, `--lifocap`), from 16 s all of it. A connection's own requests stay in order. Nothing is dropped, and the rule applies to any backlog. The trade is open: in an overload a few requests wait seconds so that most stay fast.
+- Writes commit at most once per 10 ms (`--wwin=10` in `start.sh`, group commit). A commit's WAL frames are the pages its writes dirtied, and the table's last leaf and the feed index's first leaf are in every commit, so more writes per commit means fewer frames per write. Reads don't wait for this, and every response still goes out after its commit.
+- `TCP_DEFER_ACCEPT` on the listening socket (`--defacc=1` in `start.sh`), an ordinary per-socket option that needs no privilege: a connection is handed over once its first request has arrived.
+- Also in the binary, off by default and not passed by `start.sh`: `--gate` (stops accepting while requests wait, admission control; it made things worse on our test box and stays off), `--backlog`, `--rpoll`, `--ready`, `--cc`, `--thin`, `--rcvbuf`, `--sndbuf`. They're per-socket or internal experiments, kept so the runs in our notes can be repeated.
 - SQLite's page cache comes through its public `SQLITE_CONFIG_PCACHE2` plugin API (`td_pcache` in `src/td_btree.c`, `--pcache`): an array indexed by page number, a clock instead of an LRU list. The stock cache scans its whole hash table at the next commit after each B-tree split, about 30 times a second under load at ~350 µs each, because the split parks a page under the pending-byte page number.
 - The WAL goes through a small VFS over the default one, registered with SQLite's public VFS API (`td_vfs_install` in `src/td_btree.c`, `--walbuf`). SQLite writes each frame as two `pwrite`s, a 24-byte header and a page. The shim buffers them and a commit becomes one `pwrite`, flushed before any read, truncate, sync or size call on the file, so what SQLite sees is the same. A single write stays under 128 KB, because the unix VFS masks the length with `0x1ffff` and a short write comes back as a full disk (our first 2 MB buffer did exactly that, with rollbacks and dropped connections at 200,000 users). Idea from #245 and #325, own code.
 - SQLite's page cache, the connection slab and the receive buffers ask for 2 MB pages with `madvise(MADV_HUGEPAGE)` (`--huge`). Ubuntu's default for that setting is `madvise`, so nothing on the box changes. A TLB miss under a hypervisor walks two page tables, and most of `post_view` was a binary search over scattered pages.
